{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## State at dispatch\n\nBridge DISABLED (`disabled_manually`) and stays disabled until AC1-AC7 are met.\n\n**Two analyses WITHDRAWN as non-discriminating** — recorded so nobody re-derives them as findings:\n1. Binding distinctness (5,333 bindings -> 5,333 distinct keys). The map is keyed by rebar id, so a re-create overwrites; an orphaned create is invisible. Proves the map is well-formed, nothing more.\n2. Per-title Jira-vs-rebar count match (89/89 groups equal on both sides). An INBOUND echo via `_apply_inbound_create` creates one ticket on EACH side, so it PRESERVES this match. The test's pass is what the failure mode predicts.\n\nA parallel disposition analysis reached \"no evidence of reconciler echoes\" resting partly on (2) and on characterising the reconciler signature as \"an extra Jira issue with no rebar counterpart\". That characterisation fits the OUTBOUND bugs (`21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03`; `dispatch_one.py:214` dedup JQL) but NOT the inbound path. That conclusion is therefore NOT accepted; H2 stays live pending experiment.\n\n## Data carried forward (to be re-derived, not trusted)\n\n- Predicate: store's own `normalize_title`, `src/rebar/_commands/recent_creates.py:55`. 92 colliding titles / 428 excess, of which **3 groups are normalizer FALSE POSITIVES** inside the legitimate `Condense comments in <path>` epic (folds `__init__.py`/`_init.py`, `_optionality.yml`/`optionality.yml`); all six carry distinct one-path file impact. **File impact discriminates where the title predicate does not** — directly relevant to AC7.\n- Real population: **89 groups / 425 excess / 389 open**.\n- The 389-vs-321 tension resolves: open-only counting promotes an open duplicate to apparent-canonical in the 68 groups whose true canonical is already closed. 389 - 68 = 321, and open-only yields 72 groups (the Jira-side figure). Arithmetic to be verified; AC4 requires this explanation.\n- Provenance of the 389 open targets: `python` 388 / `jira` 1. Six bursts: 08-31 06h(41) 07h(33) 22h(54) 23h(98), 09-01 00h(81) 03h(81). NOTE `creation_channel` is self-reported.\n- The single `jira`-channel open member is `jira-reb-3584`, read as an INBOUND mirror — i.e. the reconciler creating a rebar ticket from a Jira issue. That is H2's mechanism firing at least once. Best real-world specimen; benign adoption vs first link of an echo is exactly what the experiments must settle.\n- 8 members authored `rebar-bridge[bot]` are recurring `[binding-drift] bridge-fsck ...` alerts, all closed, none in the target set. AC7's detector needs an exemption path for recurring alerts.\n\n## Writes already made\n\n15 tickets closed `--class duplicate` with `duplicates` links to pre-burst canonicals, before the halt landed. All python-channel burst artifacts. No `--force` anywhere; no Jira mutation. 4 re-parent attempts were REFUSED by rebar (cannot re-parent to a closed ticket), so no re-homing occurred. **Left closed deliberately** — reversing costs more writes with no benefit until the cause is known, and their closure is not evidence about the cause.\n\n## Operator-gated, not decided by an agent\n\n`nauseating-inquiring-ibex` (95da) and `subjective-sudden-goitered` (df55) each hold legitimate non-duplicate open children whose canonical epics are closed; rebar refuses re-parenting to a closed ticket. Interim: **leave both duplicate epics OPEN** (no data loss, fully reversible). Reopening the canonical epics to adopt the children is the alternative and is the operator's call."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788636691302279349,"uuid":"7b12fc7c-9855-48bc-98e0-54321c2c2b04"}